<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/docs/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/docs/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
import Invoice from './Invoice.abide'
import InvoiceReminder from './InvoiceReminder.abide'
import Deals from './Deals.abide'
import invoiceSource from './Invoice.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import invoiceReminderSource from './InvoiceReminder.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import dealsSource from './Deals.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <p>Here you write the handler.</p>

    <Section title="A handler is an export of a `<script server>`">
        <Example
            id="rpc-call"
            title="`GET(fn)` — a handler a page imports"
            summary="An invoice read straight out of the database by a component. Reload the page and watch it land."
            files={{
                'Invoice.abide': invoiceSource,
            }}
        >
            <Invoice />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 file, 0 API in it</em> — against a route, a fetch, a client wrapper and a
        type kept in step by hand.</p>

        <p>A <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code> block runs on the server alone. Write one in any
        <code>.abide</code>, wrap a function in
        <code>GET</code> and export it. <code>GET</code> derives the argument and result schemas
        from the annotations you already wrote.</p>

        <p>The address is the directory the file sits in, then the export's name:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Export</th><th>Answers at</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>src/data/read-data-without-writing-an-api/Invoice.abide</code></td><td><code>getInvoice</code></td><td><code>/server/read-data-without-writing-an-api/getInvoice</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/users/page.abide</code></td><td><code>getUser</code></td><td><code>/users/getUser</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/layout.abide</code></td><td><code>health</code></td><td><code>/health</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>The file's own name is never part of it. Split a directory's handlers across as many
        files as you like, or keep them in the page that calls them. Two files exporting one name
        there is a build error naming both.</p>

        <p>You never write that address down. It is there so a log line or a network tab tells you
        which export you are looking at.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A page reaches the handler through an ordinary import">
        <Code language="abide" caption="src/invoices/[id]/page.abide — excerpt" code={`<script>
import { reactive, route } from '@abide/abide'
import { getInvoice } from '../Invoice.abide'

const invoice = reactive(() => getInvoice({ id: route.params.id }))
</script>

<h1>Invoice {invoice.number}</h1>
<p>{invoice.total} due {invoice.dueOn}</p>`} />

        <p>The import is real — name, argument type and return type are the ones you just wrote. No
        <code>fetch</code>, no loading boilerplate, no <code>await</code> in the markup.</p>

        <p>The page reads <code>invoice</code>, which starts the load. The document goes out
        immediately with holes where the values are. The holes fill when the row lands.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The browser receives a generated client, not your server block">
        <p>The browser never sees the <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code>. Its copy of the file
        <strong>generates</strong> one export per handler in the block's place, carrying:</p>

        <ul>
            <li>the HTTP method</li>
            <li>the mount-relative address</li>
            <li>the description</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Nothing else. Your database import and the block's own module-level work were never part
        of the module the browser got — so "no server code shipped" is how the build works, not
        something an optimizer is trusted to have achieved.</p>

        <p>A server block exports handlers alone, and anything else is a build error. Import a
        module under a <code>server</code> directory into a page and you get a compile error naming
        it. Never a stub that silently does nothing.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="You get a `Reactive`, not a promise">
        <Example
            id="rpc-reactive"
            title="A call hands back a `Reactive`, not a promise"
            summary="An invoice and the reminder button that must not fire before it lands. Wait for the total, then press Reload."
            files={{
                'InvoiceReminder.abide': invoiceReminderSource,
            }}
        >
            <InvoiceReminder />
        </Example>

        <p><code>{'getInvoiceTotal({ id })'}</code> hands back the same <code>Reactive</code> every
        <code>reactive</code> does — so the probes and the triggers are on the
        <strong>result</strong> of the call, and a promise's one answer is not what a button waiting
        on it needs.</p>

        <p><code>busy()</code> beside <code>ready()</code> tells a reload from a first load,
        <code>await invoice</code> waits, and <code>invoice.refresh()</code> reloads. That is why
        the reminder button above stays live across the second load, and goes back to disabled only
        when there is genuinely nothing held. The hand-written arm tracks two variables of its own
        to say the same thing, because a promise answers once and everything after that is the
        caller's to remember.</p>

        <p>See <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A `GET` over a `global` value follows the server">
        <Example
            id="rpc-follow"
            title="A `GET` over a `global` value follows the server"
            summary="The open deals, and a button that moves one on the server. Open this page in a second tab and press it in either: both move, and nothing on the page reloads the list."
            files={{
                'Deals.abide': dealsSource,
            }}
        >
            <Deals />
        </Example>

        <p><em>1 import, 0 sockets</em> — the arm opens a socket of its own, names a version and
        re-renders from the frames by hand.</p>

        <p>A browser that reads a <code>GET</code> stays subscribed to it over one web socket the
        whole page shares. When the handler serves a <code>global</code> value, a change the server
        makes — a <code>set</code>, a <code>patch</code>, a reload — reaches every browser holding
        it, and the list above moves with nothing on the page asking.</p>

        <p>The first read and the socket are two paths, and a change landing between them would be
        lost. So the answer names its version as an ordinary <code>ETag</code>, the browser names
        that version when it subscribes, and the server sends the value only if it has moved since
        (18.6). The same <code>ETag</code> serves any reader: a <code>GET</code> carrying
        <code>If-None-Match</code> with the version it holds is answered <code>304 Not
        Modified</code> and no body (18.19), so <code>curl</code> and a browser's cache revalidate
        the way the page does.</p>

        <p>The follow itself is standard too. Ask the same address for
        <code>text/event-stream</code> and it is a stream of server-sent events: <code>event:
        value</code> with the value and its version as the event <code>id</code>, then <code>event:
        patch</code> with each change as JSON Patch (18.20). An <code>EventSource</code>
        reconnecting sends the last <code>id</code> back as <code>Last-Event-ID</code> and is sent
        only what moved after it — so <code>curl -N -H 'Accept: text/event-stream'</code> follows a
        value with nothing abide-shaped on its side, and <code>abide watch</code> does the same from
        a shell.</p>

        <p>A trigger reaches browsers too, for any <code>GET</code>:
        <code>{'getContact.invalidate({ id })'}</code> inside a handler marks every browser's copy
        of that contact stale, whether or not the value is <code>global</code>.</p>

        <p>A <code>GET</code> declaring <code>middleware</code> is followed too. Its rungs run once,
        as the subscription opens — over the socket mux, or as the request opening an event stream —
        and a subscription they admit is fed the value like any other, while one they refuse is sent
        its triggers alone (18.8). So a signed-in chat room streams, and the decision is made per
        connection: access revoked after it opened takes effect when it next reconnects, or when the
        server ends it.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Two callers asking for one invoice are one request">
        <p>The <code>reactive</code> a handler becomes is not ceremony. A keyed value is identity
        per arguments: two components asking for invoice <code>42</code> are one request, and the
        second gets the value the first is waiting on.</p>

        <p>Name the caching yourself in the same object as the description:</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/invoices/api.abide — excerpt" code={`<script server>
export const getInvoiceTotals = GET(
    ({ id }: { id: string }) => database.invoice.totals(id),
    {
        ttl: 30_000,
        description: "One invoice's totals, by id. Cached for 30 seconds.",
    },
)
</script>`} />

        <p><code>GET</code> accepts three things:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Target</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>a keyed value</td><td>identity per args — coalescing, <code>ttl</code>, <code>invalidate</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>one <code>Reactive</code></td><td>that value, a channel among them — a client reading it follows every production (18.3)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>a plain function</td><td>sugar: <code>GET(fn, options)</code> <strong>is</strong> <code>GET(reactive(fn, options))</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>So there is no uncached endpoint to reason about.</p>

        <p>Fill in <code>description</code>. It rides onto every surface the handler generates, and
        an agent reads it to decide whether this is the call it wants.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A `GET` must not write">
        <p>The one rule the framework cannot enforce for you.</p>

        <p>The principal cookie is <code>SameSite=Lax</code>, so a top-level navigation from another
        site sends it. A <code>GET</code> that writes is reachable from an <code>&lt;a
        href&gt;</code> on a page you do not control, with your user's credentials attached.</p>

        <p>If it writes, declare it with
        <a href={url('/data/change-something-on-the-server')}><code>POST</code></a>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/data/refuse-a-request-and-say-why')}>Failures</a> — when the row is
            missing, or not theirs</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/check-what-callers-send-you')}>Schemas</a> — declaring the
            shape instead of deriving it</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a> — a rung in
            front of this</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
